 \paragraph{Contributions.}
 \begin{enumerate}
-\item \textbf{Problem framing.} Label-free TTA as a certified \emph{adapt/freeze/abstain} decision on the sign of the adaptation benefit.
-\item \textbf{Exact frontier.} The benefit sign is label-free identifiable \emph{iff} $|M|>\beta$; below the frontier, matched evidence forces abstention (Theorems~\ref{lem:nonid},~\ref{thm:headline}; \S\ref{sec:theory}).
-\item \textbf{Finite-sample certificate.} \textsc{KGA} controls the unconditional false-adapt/false-freeze rate at level $\alpha$ under stated coverage (Theorem~\ref{thm:certificate}); existing label-free estimators can be read as its $\beta{=}0$ plug-in, committing under an implicit negligible-drift assumption.
-\item \textbf{Drop-in method.} \textsc{KGA} wraps Tent/EATA/SAR without changing their adaptation objectives (Algorithm~\ref{alg:kga}; \S\ref{sec:method}).
-\item \textbf{Uniform empirical accounting.} A nine-track panel reports the same metrics, split logic, and evidence tier for every benchmark; only locked or reconciled results are promoted to empirical claims (\S\ref{sec:exp}).
+\item \textbf{Adapt/freeze/abstain formulation.} We make the target risk difference, strict actions, fallback semantics, and target-label-free information boundary explicit.
+\item \textbf{Exact strict-commitment frontier.} A strict adapt/freeze action is uniformly sound iff $|M|>\beta$; the interior and boundary cases are proved separately (Theorem~\ref{thm:headline}).
+\item \textbf{Coverage-based finite-sample certificate.} Under measurable interval coverage, the empirical rule controls marginal false-adapt and false-freeze events (Theorem~\ref{thm:certificate}).
+\item \textbf{KGA deployable wrapper.} KGA wraps Tent/EATA/SAR without changing their adaptation objectives and uses shadow state, commit, rollback, and logging (Algorithm~\ref{alg:kga}).
+\item \textbf{Regime-based empirical evaluation.} A uniform panel separates controlled mixed wins, natural no-harm results, constructed routing, and weak-evidence diagnostics.
